490135 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490135 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490135 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490135 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490135 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186489759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490135 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6bfed8e4-2eb5-53f3-a595-09aab6d29d72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490149 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490149 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490149 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490149 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490149 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490149 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490149 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490149 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186489680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490149 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "81a5601c-846e-50b6-b119-42985d6b94c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490198 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490198 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490198 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490198 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490198 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490198 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490198 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490198 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186490031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490198 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eb0da733-cf1a-5325-b833-cf95bff8f2c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490230 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490230 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96590" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490230 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490230 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $A$ is symmetric, then ${A}^{2}$ is always symmetric.\n\nII. The sum of *any* two $n\\times n$ matrices is always symmetric.\n\nIII. If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490230 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "30380c91-5652-5051-8810-4fc9abb6a29f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490230 #edb/ref 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(AA)^{T} & ={A}^{T}{A}^{T} \\\\  & =AA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their sum is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any square matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490264 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490264 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143682" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490264 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490264 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}4 & \\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490264 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7243696d-a88c-5019-9844-41fc2be79db7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490264 #edb/ref 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}4 & \\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490309 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490309 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93990" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490309 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490309 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490309 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d897469f-7656-5297-824e-0adce50d0f86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490309 #edb/ref 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P,$ $Q,$ and $R.$\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490315 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490315 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93986" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490315 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490315 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 5 & \\sqrt{2} & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490315 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e09bb1cf-2314-567d-8f07-99c777c75c71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490315 #edb/ref 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{12}\\ne {q}_{21},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$R$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
